{"type":"assistant","message":{"content":[{"thinking":"The roadmap checks out—all the hard requirements pass, and the 24-module warning is just a soft advisory. The dimensions and font size are good. Now I'm setting up the four flowcharts with a vertical layout, left-to-right flow, and edges that loop back to the starting point. The actual content structure will depend on what each problem's logic demands, so I'm starting with the first one.

Q1 is linear with a natural parallel structure—the temperature and concentration fields decouple, so they can solve simultaneously before merging to output the tables. Q2 involves iteration with a Picard convergence loop inside each time step, so it needs a vertical layout with a feedback edge. Q3 reuses Q2's model but adds a threshold check on the concentration to decide whether to continue advancing or stop, creating another decision loop. Q4 transforms to material coordinates using the moving boundary, then checks convergence at each step before interpolating back to physical coordinates for output. Each flowchart captures its distinct logic—parallel decoupling, inner iteration, threshold feedback, and coordinate transformation.

